Paragraph One
Point Of Difference
An important difference between great powers and superpowers is the extent of their military
capabilities and their ability to project military power across the world.
Great Powers
Great powers possess significant military strength and ability to both defend themselves and
project power in their regions. However, they lack the ability to act across different regions of
the world at the same time.
For example, the United Kingdom is considered a great power due to its advanced military
capabilities, including nuclear weapons and one of the worldʼs most modern navies.
However, its defence budget of £50 billion and limited troop numbers limit its ability to
project power globally, as seen in its reliance on alliances like NATO for major military
operations, such as in Afghanistan and Libya.
