By contrast, superpowers have far greater military capabilities that allow them to operate
anywhere in the world, projecting power through advanced technology, large-scale military
operations, and strategic alliances. Superpowers frequently intervene in international conflicts
and maintain a global presence through bases and fleets.
For example, the United States spends over $900 billion annually on its military, making up
40% of global defence spending. With over 700 military bases across the world, it has the
capacity to quickly respond to crises worldwide, which is a crucial deterrent to great
powers and rogue states in different regions of the world at the same time.
Paragraph Two
Point Of Difference
Another difference between great powers and superpowers is their position in the global
economy, particularly in advanced and geopolitically important sectors.
Superpowers
Superpowers dominate the global economy, often setting the rules of trade and investment
through their structural power. They form a significant portion of global GDP and are typically
leaders in advanced, geopolitically significant industries such as technology, aerospace, and
energy.
For example, during the Cold War the USSR was an economic superpower as it controlled
the economies of Eastern Europe and had significant structural influence as one of the
worldʼs largest oil and natural gas exporters.
In the 1980s, the USSR produced over 12 million barrels of oil per day, allowing it to exert
significant economic leverage over its satellite states and challenge Western dominance by
investing in its military, the arms race and other areas.
Great Powers
By contrast, great powers, while economically influential, do not wield the same level of
structural power globally. They may have significant influence in one area of the global
economy, but arenʼt as strong as superpowers and therefore arenʼt able to use their economic
power to influence other countries to the same extent.
For example, Germany is a great power with a highly developed economy and a leading role
in the automotive and manufacturing industries, yet it lacks the broader structural power of
the United States. Its economic influence is largely concentrated within Europe and it isnʼt
as powerful in other areas such as technology and energy.
Paragraph Three
Point Of Difference
A final important difference between great powers and superpowers is that the latter often have
significant structural power and soft power influence that allow them to dominate international
institutions and shape global agreements.
Great Powers
Great powers play a significant role in global governance and diplomacy and do have influence
within international governmental organisations such as the United Nations, however they oftenExamine The Differences Between Great Powers and Superpowers In Global Politics. 12 Politics Explained 12 Marker Plan2
have to follow the interests of superpowers and canʼt unilaterally shape the rules and norms of
the international order.
For example, France is a permanent member of the UN Security Council and also has
influence as a major contributor to NATO operations. However, it is within the United Statesʼ
orbit and rarely makes decisions that the U.S. doesnʼt support.
Superpowers
Superpowers have far more influence over the international system. They use their structural
and soft power to influence other countries and ensure that global rules and institutions are in
their interests and maintain their dominance.
For instance, the United States leverages its leadership in institutions like the IMF, World
Bank, and UN to promote liberal economic policies, democratic governance and limit the
influence of Russia and China.
Its cultural dominance, through Hollywood, global brands like Apple and McDonaldʼs, and
platforms like Netflix, further extends its soft power by shaping global consumer habits and
perceptions of the American way of life.
This combination of structural and cultural influence allows superpowers to dominate global
politics in a way that great powers cannot.
